 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The preconditioner computation involves Jacobian evaluation ($O(m \cdot k \cdot \text{proj\_cost})$), covariance calculation ($O(m \cdot k^2)$), and matrix square root inversion ($O(k^3)$). Since $k$ is small (typically 7-10) and $m$ is moderate, this is negligible compared to NeRF training. The preconditioner is computed once.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Stores $P_i^{-1}$ for each camera ($O(N \cdot k^2)$). This is minimal.
-*   **Performance:** Improves convergence and accuracy in camera-optimizing NeRFs, particularly for challenging parameterizations like SE3 with focal length [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles mixed units and correlations effectively. Simple to implement on top of existing NeRF frameworks.
-*   **Limitations:** Preconditioner is static; may become suboptimal if camera estimates deviate significantly from initialization. Not suitable for dynamic scene updates without recomputation (which breaks Adam's momentum) [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When optimizing camera poses and intrinsics jointly with NeRF parameters, especially when parameters have different scales or units.
-*   **When not to use:** When camera poses are fixed and known accurately, or when dynamic updates to the preconditioner are required for non-stationary optimization landscapes [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The preconditioner computation involves Jacobian evaluation and matrix square root inversion, which is $O(m k^2)$ for $m$ points and $k$ parameters. This is done once at initialization. The per-step cost is dominated by rendering and gradient computation, with an additional $O(k^2)$ matrix-vector multiplication per camera per step.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the preconditioner matrix $P_i^{-1}$ for each camera, which is $O(N k^2)$ for $N$ cameras.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Improves convergence and accuracy in camera-optimizing NeRFs compared to naive optimization. It handles mixed units and correlations effectively. [S1]
+*   **Strengths:** Decorrelates parameters, normalizes scale, handles mixed units, improves convergence. [S1]
+*   **Limitations:** Preconditioner is static (computed at initialization), which may become suboptimal as camera estimates deviate. Dynamic updates are avoided to preserve Adam's gradient moment estimates. [S1]
+*   **When to Use:** When optimizing camera poses in NeRFs, especially with mixed units (e.g., focal length in pixels, translation in world units) or correlated parameters. [S1]
+*   **When Not to Use:** When computational resources are extremely limited and the overhead of computing the preconditioner is prohibitive, or when camera poses are known with high accuracy and do not require optimization. [S1]
 
 ## Background
-Camera Preconditioning was proposed by Kevin Park et al. in 2023 [S1]. It was implemented on top of ZipNeRF [Barron et al. 2023] and uses a coarse-to-fine strategy. The method draws inspiration from numerical methods for ill-conditioned problems, specifically Zero Component Analysis (ZCA) whitening [Kessy et al. 2018]. It addresses issues observed in naive camera parameterizations where perturbations affect projection significantly more along some axes than others, and where parameters are correlated [S1].
+Camera Preconditioning was proposed by Kevin Park et al. in 2023, published in ACM Transactions on Graphics [S1]. It addresses issues in NeRF optimization where camera parameters are ill-conditioned due to different units and correlations. The method draws inspiration from numerical methods and ZCA whitening. It is implemented on top of ZipNeRF [Barron et al. 2023] and uses a coarse-to-fine strategy. [S1]
 
